:question/id #uuid "346f5335-950f-5d84-8ef1-647026f99b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-51543"} {:db/id 17592186560974 :question/id #uuid "0d53c105-6357-5aa5-b9db-2ac9c9ee796f" :question/math-academy-id "q-45061"} {:db/id 17592186561053 :question/id #uuid "df1d75ef-4d29-5e1f-bd95-e67ad6f7bd69" :question/math-academy-id "q-45060"} {:db/id 17592186049803} {:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186069578} :activity/id #uuid "09fac72c-e8cf-5881-9f87-6135d15c419b" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186048652 :topic/id #uuid "b27952f8-4bda-51a9-b51b-57fe61de4a72" :topic/math-academy-id 470} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186069578 :step/content {:db/id 17592186070022 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule that takes a number as **input** and then **outputs** another number.\n\nFor instance, let's look at the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x) = 3x+1\n$$\n\nIn this example,\n\n- $f$ is the **name** of the function,\n\n- the number $x$ is the **input**,\n\n- the number $3x+1$ is the **output**.\n\nIf we **substitute** a number into our function in place of $x$, we get another number as output. \n\nFor example, by substituting $x={\\color{blue}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{2}}) = 3\\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}}+1 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{blue}{2}}$ results in an output of $7.$\n\nWe can substitute any number we like into this function! For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{-2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{-2}}) = 3\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}})+1 = -5.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{red}{-2}}$ results in an output of $-5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "902a3802-7423-5fa0-9cf1-43856055c65c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8783 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "726a2782-726c-5f7e-b8e5-138b8563ebff" :step/math-academy-id 138156 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069579}} {:db/id 17592186069579 :step/content {:db/id 17592186068701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068486 :question/id #uuid "38228952-21ef-5caf-9805-76e42ebf0caa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048594} {:db/id 17592186049299} {:db/id 17592186050127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069041} {:db/id 17592186069115} {:db/id 17592186069123} {:db/id 17592186233385} {:db/id 17592186233396} {:db/id 17592186233405} {:db/id 17592186233561} {:db/id 17592186233568} {:db/id 17592186233575} {:db/id 17592186482561} {:db/id 17592186482574} {:db/id 17592186492831} {:db/id 17592186492850} {:db/id 17592186492901} {:db/id 17592186522723}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function"} :step/id #uuid "8af30817-9be7-537a-9bfb-2ce31b525422" :step/math-academy-id 138157 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069580}} {:db/id 17592186069580 :step/content {:db/id 17592186070023 :tutorial/content "Functions with more than one input are called **multivariable functions**. \n\nFor example, the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x-y\n$$\n\ntakes in two numbers, $x$ and $y$, and returns their difference. \n\nFor multivariable functions, the order of the inputs matters. For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{blue}{3}}$ and $y={\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{3}},{\\color{red}{2}}) = {\\color{blue}{3}}-{\\color{red}{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nwhereas if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{3}},$ we get a different output:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{3}})={\\color{red}{2}}-{\\color{blue}{3}}=-1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bef6d6f-bda2-56dd-badb-0001ca2e4329" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8784 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Functions of More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "004a3461-1ea4-5e28-807b-fea6c51ae3d4" :step/math-academy-id 138161 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069581}} {:db/id 17592186069581 :step/content {:db/id 17592186068702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068487 :question/id #uuid "4e06fa0b-f3f0-5e60-9a83-67a393b4cc07" :question/math-academy-id "e-14150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b5f42de-fad6-54b6-90e1-205fdbe8a456" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069042} {:db/id 17592186069116} {:db/id 17592186069124} {:db/id 17592186482526} {:db/id 17592186482569} {:db/id 17592186492852} {:db/id 17592186544826} {:db/id 17592186544830} {:db/id 17592186544841}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "e7679093-39bc-5822-a013-8ae88e7f542e" :step/math-academy-id 138162}] :activity/title "Introduction to Functions" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186068995} {:db/id 17592186069578 :step/content {:db/id 17592186070022 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule that takes a number as **input** and then **outputs** another number.\n\nFor instance, let's look at the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x) = 3x+1\n$$\n\nIn this example,\n\n- $f$ is the **name** of the function,\n\n- the number $x$ is the **input**,\n\n- the number $3x+1$ is the **output**.\n\nIf we **substitute** a number into our function in place of $x$, we get another number as output. \n\nFor example, by substituting $x={\\color{blue}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{2}}) = 3\\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}}+1 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{blue}{2}}$ results in an output of $7.$\n\nWe can substitute any number we like into this function! For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{-2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{-2}}) = 3\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}})+1 = -5.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{red}{-2}}$ results in an output of $-5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "902a3802-7423-5fa0-9cf1-43856055c65c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8783 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "726a2782-726c-5f7e-b8e5-138b8563ebff" :step/math-academy-id 138156 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069579}} {:db/id 17592186070022 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule that takes a number as **input** and then **outputs** another number.\n\nFor instance, let's look at the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x) = 3x+1\n$$\n\nIn this example,\n\n- $f$ is the **name** of the function,\n\n- the number $x$ is the **input**,\n\n- the number $3x+1$ is the **output**.\n\nIf we **substitute** a number into our function in place of $x$, we get another number as output. \n\nFor example, by substituting $x={\\color{blue}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{2}}) = 3\\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}}+1 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{blue}{2}}$ results in an output of $7.$\n\nWe can substitute any number we like into this function! For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{-2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{-2}}) = 3\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}})+1 = -5.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{red}{-2}}$ results in an output of $-5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "902a3802-7423-5fa0-9cf1-43856055c65c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8783 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186069579 :step/content {:db/id 17592186068701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068486 :question/id #uuid "38228952-21ef-5caf-9805-76e42ebf0caa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048594} {:db/id 17592186049299} {:db/id 17592186050127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069041} {:db/id 17592186069115} {:db/id 17592186069123} {:db/id 17592186233385} {:db/id 17592186233396} {:db/id 17592186233405} {:db/id 17592186233561} {:db/id 17592186233568} {:db/id 17592186233575} {:db/id 17592186482561} {:db/id 17592186482574} {:db/id 17592186492831} {:db/id 17592186492850} {:db/id 17592186492901} {:db/id 17592186522723}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function"} :step/id #uuid "8af30817-9be7-537a-9bfb-2ce31b525422" :step/math-academy-id 138157 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069580}} {:db/id 17592186069580 :step/content {:db/id 17592186070023 :tutorial/content "Functions with more than one input are called **multivariable functions**. \n\nFor example, the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x-y\n$$\n\ntakes in two numbers, $x$ and $y$, and returns their difference. \n\nFor multivariable functions, the order of the inputs matters. For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{blue}{3}}$ and $y={\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{3}},{\\color{red}{2}}) = {\\color{blue}{3}}-{\\color{red}{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nwhereas if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{3}},$ we get a different output:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{3}})={\\color{red}{2}}-{\\color{blue}{3}}=-1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bef6d6f-bda2-56dd-badb-0001ca2e4329" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8784 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Functions of More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "004a3461-1ea4-5e28-807b-fea6c51ae3d4" :step/math-academy-id 138161 :step/next {:db/id 17592186069581}} {:db/id 17592186070023 :tutorial/content "Functions with more than one input are called **multivariable functions**. \n\nFor example, the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x-y\n$$\n\ntakes in two numbers, $x$ and $y$, and returns their difference. \n\nFor multivariable functions, the order of the inputs matters. For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{blue}{3}}$ and $y={\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{3}},{\\color{red}{2}}) = {\\color{blue}{3}}-{\\color{red}{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nwhereas if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{3}},$ we get a different output:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{3}})={\\color{red}{2}}-{\\color{blue}{3}}=-1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bef6d6f-bda2-56dd-badb-0001ca2e4329" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8784 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Functions of More Than One Variable"} {:db/id 17592186069581 :step/content {:db/id 17592186068702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186068487 :question/id #uuid "4e06fa0b-f3f0-5e60-9a83-67a393b4cc07" :question/math-academy-id "e-14150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b5f42de-fad6-54b6-90e1-205fdbe8a456" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186069042} {:db/id 17592186069116} {:db/id 1