follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b+25 & =0 \\\\ 5b & =-25 \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-5$ into equation $(1)$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-5) & =6a+14 \\\\ -10 & =6a+14 \\\\ -10-14 & =6a \\\\ 6a & =-24 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=-5.$ So,\n\n$$\na+b=-4+(-5)=-4-5=-9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca7de6ad-e459-52ba-851d-06dd86cc6059" :step/next {:db/id 17592186547164}} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b85cb2c-460d-5f91-86c0-674d13fae83e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id 17592186546309} {:answer/id #uuid "242eed59-c937-5dab-b478-b3a1accb9d4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186546329} {:answer/id #uuid "463e0a8b-5e11-54a2-8cdd-72b176626176" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186546407} {:answer/id #uuid "6a785a85-c477-5db9-a10d-570f963f9181" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186546485} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ff80ce-ffb0-542a-ba50-ce8557612884" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186546805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186546309} :answer-field/id #uuid "e282d81b-1bfe-5c5b-abe6-d91779057814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186546940} {:answer/id #uuid "1b85cb2c-460d-5f91-86c0-674d13fae83e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id 17592186546309} {:answer/id #uuid "242eed59-c937-5dab-b478-b3a1accb9d4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186546329} {:answer/id #uuid "463e0a8b-5e11-54a2-8cdd-72b176626176" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186546407} {:answer/id #uuid "6a785a85-c477-5db9-a10d-570f963f9181" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186546485} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ff80ce-ffb0-542a-ba50-ce8557612884" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186546805} {:db/id 17592186547164 :step/content {:db/id 17592186546971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d7a53c-5916-5db7-992b-ff7a626a51c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186546336} {:answer/id #uuid "51399b6f-2763-5014-9b02-635633f0dada" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186546427} {:answer/id #uuid "9e23c320-e17b-534f-8a91-354568ebc163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186546603} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f272eb-ffc1-5fb6-b622-055a0def12d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186546617} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f37f2b-79d8-5ebd-8615-8e1bcfb28380" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186546732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186546732} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ca5c4c-b502-5598-a6f0-d81d606b319e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186546881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ad70587-af92-55e0-95cf-31aa82c10e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-52115" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a-b?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a=7 \\\\ 3a+6b=15\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the first equation is already solved for $a:$\n\n$$\na=7\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=7$ into $3a+6b=15,$ then we can find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+6b & =15 \\\\ 3(7)+6b & =15 \\\\ 6b & =-6 \\\\ b & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=7,\\,b=-1.$ So,\n\n$$\na-b=7-(-1)=7+1=8.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5c9a09f-e906-5432-9fef-4ec9ba80344e"} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d7a53c-5916-5db7-992b-ff7a626a51c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186546336} {:answer/id #uuid "51399b6f-2763-5014-9b02-635633f0dada" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186546427} {:answer/id #uuid "9e23c320-e17b-534f-8a91-354568ebc163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186546603} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f272eb-ffc1-5fb6-b622-055a0def12d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186546617} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f37f2b-79d8-5ebd-8615-8e1bcfb28380" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186546732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id 17592186546732} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ca5c4c-b502-5598-a6f0-d81d606b319e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493} :db/id 17592186546881} {:answer/id #uuid "25d7a53c-5916-5db7-992b-ff7a626a51c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186546336} {:answer/id #uuid "51399b6f-2763-5014-9b02-635633f0dada" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186546427} {:answer/id #uuid "9e23c320-e17b-534f-8a91-354568ebc163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186546603} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f272eb-ffc1-5fb6-b622-055a0def12d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186546617} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f37f2b-79d8-5ebd-8615-8e1bcfb28380" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186546732} {:activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186055608} :activity/id #uuid "581ada9e-b8f9-5513-9a35-8ae3af36139e" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049074 :topic/id #uuid "d85c70bd-27ec-54bb-a459-275d176f6b8b" :topic/math-academy-id 89} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186055608 :step/content {:db/id 17592186056069 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + 3x + 4x_(⏟)_(7x) + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90a8aa34-4549-57c2-bdc2-24cc962cd306" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8891 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "add682be-a6dd-51b0-a261-ba8a3f84b958" :step/math-academy-id 178072 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055609}} {:db/id 17592186055609 :step/content {:db/id 17592186054658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186054476 :question/id #uuid "1e0116a2-6f90-5b57-97c1-6a64b45c9cef" :question/math-academy-id "e-14343"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c9ba00d-9171-5732-8539-2e2943cce58e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048351} {:db/id 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186055108} {:db/id 17592186055495} {:db/id 17592186055497} {:db/id 17592186233563} {:db/id 17592186233570} {:db/id 17592186233577}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Positive Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "072ef290-5a3e-575d-a0fd-7d8084343f23" :step/math-academy-id 178073 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055610}} {:db/id 17592186055610 :step/content {:db/id 17592186056070 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 8\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term $(-8)$ is *negative*. So, when writing down our factor pairs, one number will always be positive, and the other will always be negative.\n\nSo, first, we write down all factor pairs of $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-8), (-1, 8), (2,-4), (-2, 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-8) &= -7 & \\cdot \\\\ (-1) + 8|= 7 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + (-4)|=-2 & ✓ \\\\ (-2) + 4|= 2 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-2x = 2x-4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (-4x - 8) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 2) - 4(x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b5a5752-232b-5714-b74a-1e50c4210b83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8894 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Constants"} :step/id #uuid "232a2780-b6bf-56f2-b607-6b8d27ae3f25" :step/math-academy-id 178077 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055611}} {:db/id 17592186055611 :step/content {:db/id 17592186054659 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186054478 :question/id #uuid "41c3e610-0259-59db-9494-3af6f47c7360" :question/math-academy-id "e-14350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac258511-5028-5fc8-9843-99309b36a38e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186055130} {:db/id 17592186055229} {:db/id 17592186055231} {:db/id 17592186459240} {:db/id 17592186480700} {:db/id 17592186487383} {:db/id 17592186491186} {:db/id 17592186498516}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Constants"} :step/id #uuid "47e26748-840d-518f-8679-595bc9b0406d" :step/math-academy-id 178078 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055612}} {:db/id 17592186055612 :step/content {:db/id 17592186056076 :tutorial/content "Remember that to factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the constant term that adds to the coefficient of the linear term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 7x+12\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the constant term $(12)$ is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the constant is *positive* and the coefficient of the linear term is *negative*, we must first consider the *negative* factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$. These are as follows:\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\qquad (-2,-6),\\qquad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their *sum* is negative, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) + (-12) &= -13 & \\cdot \\\\ (-2) + (-6)|=-8 & \\cdot \\\\ (-3) + (-4)|=-7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-7x = -3x - 4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 7x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 3x) + (-4x + 12) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3) + 4(-x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to get a *common* factor in both terms, we take an additional factor of $-1$ from the second term:\n\n$$\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3)\n$$\n\nNow that we have a common factor of $(x-3)$, we factor the expression fully in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 3)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25f481d5-38d8-5f35-95fc-dd74f97d0394" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9504 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} :step/id #uuid "47589875-8a7f-5cea-a2cf-0da701bb35ac" :step/math-academy-id 178082 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055613}} {:db/id 17592186055613 :step/content {:db/id 17592186054660 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186054477 :question/id #uuid "f51ebe90-e58a-5712-84b8-4367ed18d58e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14346"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65a95770-9419-5a8f-b264-58cca3e3c0db" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186055109} {:db/id 17592186055131} {:db/id 17592186055496}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} :step/id #uuid "87945626-63e1-5990-b1f8-6d13138cf85c" :step/math-academy-id 178083}] :activity/title "Factoring Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186054931} {:db/id 17592186055608 :step/content {:db/id 17592186056069 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + 3x + 4x_(⏟)_(7x) + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90a8aa34-4549-57c2-bdc2-24cc962cd306" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8891 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "add682be-a6dd-51b0-a261-ba8a3f84b958" :step/math-academy-id 178072 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055609}} {:db/id 17592186056069 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + 3x + 4x_(⏟)_(7x) + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90a8aa34-4549-57c2-bdc2-24cc962cd306" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8891 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186055609 :step/content {:db/id 17592186054658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186054476 :question/id #uuid "1e0116a2-6f90-5b57-97c1-6a64b45c9cef" :question/math-academy-id "e-14343"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c9ba00d-9171-5732-8539-2e2943cce58e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048351} {:db/id 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186055108} {:db/id 17592186055495} {:db/id 17592186055497} {:db/id 17592186233563} {:db/id 17592186233570} {:db/id 17592186233577}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Positive Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "072ef290-5a3e-575d-a0fd-7d8084343f23" :step/math-academy-id 178073 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055610}} {:db/id 17592186055610 :step/content {:db/id 17592186056070 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 8\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term $(-8)$ is *negative*. So, when writing down our factor pairs, one number will always be positive, and the other will always be negative.\n\nSo, first, we write down all factor pairs of $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-8), (-1, 8), (2,-4), (-2, 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-8) &= -7 & \\cdot \\\\ (-1) + 8|= 7 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + (-4)|=-2 & ✓ \\\\ (-2) + 4|= 2 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-2x = 2x-4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (-4x - 8) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 2) - 4(x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b5a5752-232b-5714-b74a-1e50c4210b83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8894 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Constants"} :step/id #uuid "232a2780-b6bf-56f2-b607-6b8d27ae3f25" :step/math-academy-id 178077 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055611}} {:db/id 17592186056070 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 8\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term $(-8)$ is *negative*. So, when writing down our factor pairs, one number will always be positive, and the other will always be negative.\n\nSo, first, we write down all factor pairs of $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-8), (-1, 8), (2,-4), (-2, 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-8) &= -7 & \\cdot \\\\ (-1) + 8|= 7 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + (-4)|=-2 & ✓ \\\\ (-2) + 4|= 2 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-2x = 2x-4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (-4x - 8) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 2) - 4(x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b5a5752-232b-5714-b74a-1e50c4210b83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8894 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Constants"} {:db/id 17592186055611 :step/content {:db/id 17592186054659 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186054478 :question/id #uuid "41c3e610-0259-59db-9494-3af6f47c7360" :question/math-academy-id "e-14350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac258511-5028-5fc8-9843-99309b36a38e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186055130} {:db/id 17592186055229} {:db/id 17592186055231} {:db/id 17592186459240} {:db/id 17592186480700} {:db/id 17592186487383} {:db/id 17592186491186} {:db/id 17592186498516}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Constants"} :step/id #uuid "47e26748-840d-518f-8679-595bc9b0406d" :step/math-academy-id 178078 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055612}} {:db/id 17592186055612 :step/content {:db/id 17592186056076 :tutorial/content "Remember that to factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the constant term that adds to the coefficient of the linear term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 7x+12\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the constant term $(12)$ is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the constant is *positive* and the coefficient of the linear term is *negative*, we must first consider the *negative* factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$. These are as follows:\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\qquad (-2,-6),\\qquad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their *sum* is negative, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) + (-12) &= -13 & \\cdot \\\\ (-2) + (-6)|=-8 & \\cdot \\\\ (-3) + (-4)|=-7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-7x = -3x - 4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 7x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 3x) + (-4x + 12) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3) + 4(-x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to get a *common* factor in both terms, we take an additional factor of $-1$ from the second term:\n\n$$\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3)\n$$\n\nNow that we have a common factor of $(x-3)$, we factor the expression fully in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 3)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25f481d5-38d8-5f35-95fc-dd74f97d0394" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9504 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} :step/id #uuid "47589875-8a7f-5cea-a2cf-0da701bb35ac" :step/math-academy-id 178082 :step/next {:db/id 17592186055613}} {:db/id 17592186056076 :tutorial/content "Remember that to factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the constant term that adds to the coefficient of the linear term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 7x+12\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the constant term $(12)$ is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the constant is *positive* and the coefficient of the linear term is *negative*, we must first consider the *negative* factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$. These are as follows:\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\qquad (-2,-6),\\qquad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their *sum* is negative, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) + (-12) &= -13 & \\cdot \\\\ (-2) + (-6)|=-8 & \\cdot \\\\ (-3) + (-4)|=-7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-7x = -3x - 4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 7x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 3x) + (-4x + 12) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3) + 4(-x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to get a *common* factor in both terms, we take an additional factor of $-1$ from the second term:\n\n$$\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3)\n$$\n\nNow that we have a common factor of $(x-3)$, we factor the expression fully in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 3)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25f481d5-38d8-5f35-95fc-dd74f97d0394" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9504 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} {:db/id 17592186055613 :step/content {:db/id 17592186054660 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186054477 :question/id #uuid "f51ebe90-e58a-5712-84b8-4367ed18d58e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14346"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65a95770-9419-5a8f-b264-58cca3e3c0db" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186055109} {:db/id 17592186055131} {:db/id 17592186055496}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} :step/id #uuid "87945626-63e1-5990-b1f8-6d13138cf85c" :step/math-academy-id 178083} {:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186134608} :activity/id #uuid "25739194-82c1-5788-9ee1-b6fee61cbd79" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049399 :topic/id #uuid "f1aa8ae2-23cf-5d91-8af0-a43e9db5e668" :topic/math-academy-id 2214} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186134608 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135191 :tutorial/content "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nEvery arithmetic sequence can also be written using the following recursive formula:\n\n![](images/a2/a2d28772cb4cb6078d2fccdca054ec1c2dccf27a5ea0b5972b9ac174eaad671f.svg)\n\nFor example, suppose that we're given the following explicit formula for an arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\na_n =2n+6, \\quad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo convert to a recursive formula, we just need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)+6=8 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)+6=10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}8}$ and the common difference is $d={\\color{red}2}.$ Therefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}2}, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}8}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6cd14c7-922b-5e05-9c8e-b842717155ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8910 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02ae2395-79da-5dc3-9f17-a75936abdc07" :step/math-academy-id 191930 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134609}} {:db/id 17592186134609 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133531 :question/id #uuid "00113d76-b950-513e-a3ea-29eef91c9cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-14383"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b8b10a8-f81a-5dae-b54e-b10959e3ad14" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186050157} {:db/id 17592186050380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134163} {:db/id 17592186134446} {:db/id 17592186560987} {:db/id 17592186561011}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula"} :step/id #uuid "ed82570d-8cc5-564d-8da6-d86c3395fced" :step/math-academy-id 191931 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134610}} {:db/id 17592186134610 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133532 :question/id #uuid "e04ad3e0-888e-51c0-b60c-e816b7df514b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14386"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d36a3a61-e23b-5ce3-ac65-f7ac1691ec4e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134177} {:db/id 17592186134447} {:db/id 17592186560975} {:db/id 17592186561005}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "340d50dc-babe-5074-be5e-52e39f437e88" :step/math-academy-id 191934 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134611}} {:db/id 17592186134611 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135192 :tutorial/content "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the arithmetic sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}1},\\, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}6},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1?\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}6},$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =6+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =7-6 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $a_1 = {\\color{blue}6}$ and $d={\\color{red}1}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n&= {\\color{blue}6} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}1}) \\[3pt]\n& =6 + n-1\\[3pt]\n&=n+5\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=n+5, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c5b9f8-4c71-5c1f-8841-3f22646e5074" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8914 :tutorial/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7cd53e1d-4c0c-570b-bef4-68f8081c2fef" :step/math-academy-id 191938 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134612}} {:db/id 17592186134612 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133533 :question/id #uuid "d732cfb5-9db5-535d-adb1-5d3292f1c497" :question/math-academy-id "e-14389"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e1e439-ec4a-5577-80d5-3b113bf36e74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134056} {:db/id 17592186134164} {:db/id 17592186233564} {:db/id 17592186233571} {:db/id 17592186233578} {:db/id 17592186560995} {:db/id 17592186561033}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "2a238f59-a780-5248-bfa5-a217bec91d6c" :step/math-academy-id 191939 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134613}} {:db/id 17592186134613 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133534 :question/id #uuid "26b3b89d-d485-51ac-b964-8377ce3aff28" :question/math-academy-id "e-14393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baac268a-629e-5887-885a-49393c72776f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134178} {:db/id 17592186134479} {:db/id 17592186560974} {:db/id 17592186561053}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "c81f0b63-4b36-56b0-bc0f-1535d35453ef" :step/math-academy-id 191943}] :activity/title "Translating Between Explicit and Recursive Formulas for Arithmetic Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186134049} {:db/id 17592186134608 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135191 :tutorial/content "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nEvery arithmetic sequence can also be written using the following recursive formula:\n\n![](images/a2/a2d28772cb4cb6078d2fccdca054ec1c2dccf27a5ea0b5972b9ac174eaad671f.svg)\n\nFor example, suppose that we're given the following explicit formula for an arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\na_n =2n+6, \\quad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo convert to a recursive formula, we just need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)+6=8 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)+6=10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}8}$ and the common difference is $d={\\color{red}2}.$ Therefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}2}, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}8}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6cd14c7-922b-5e05-9c8e-b842717155ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8910 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02ae2395-79da-5dc3-9f17-a75936abdc07" :step/math-academy-id 191930 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134609}} {:db/id 17592186135191 :tutorial/content "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nEvery arithmetic sequence can also be written using the following recursive formula:\n\n![](images/a2/a2d28772cb4cb6078d2fccdca054ec1c2dccf27a5ea0b5972b9ac174eaad671f.svg)\n\nFor example, suppose that we're given the following explicit formula for an arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\na_n =2n+6, \\quad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo convert to a recursive formula, we just need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)+6=8 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)+6=10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}8}$ and the common difference is $d={\\color{red}2}.$ Therefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}2}, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}8}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6cd14c7-922b-5e05-9c8e-b842717155ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8910 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186134609 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133531 :question/id #uuid "00113d76-b950-513e-a3ea-29eef91c9cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-14383"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b8b10a8-f81a-5dae-b54e-b10959e3ad14" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186050157} {:db/id 17592186050380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134163} {:db/id 17592186134446} {:db/id 17592186560987} {:db/id 17592186561011}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula"} :step/id #uuid "ed82570d-8cc5-564d-8da6-d86c3395fced" :step/math-academy-id 191931 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134610}} {:db/id 17592186134610 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133532 :question/id #uuid "e04ad3e0-888e-51c0-b60c-e816b7df514b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14386"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d36a3a61-e23b-5ce3-ac65-f7ac1691ec4e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134177} {:db/id 17592186134447} {:db/id 17592186560975} {:db/id 17592186561005}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "340d50dc-babe-5074-be5e-52e39f437e88" :step/math-academy-id 191934 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134611}} {:db/id 17592186134611 :step/content {:db/id 17592186135192 :tutorial/content "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the arithmetic sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}1},\\, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}6},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1?\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}6},$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =6+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =7-6 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $a_1 = {\\color{blue}6}$ and $d={\\color{red}1}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n&= {\\color{blue}6} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}1}) \\[3pt]\n& =6 + n-1\\[3pt]\n&=n+5\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=n+5, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c5b9f8-4c71-5c1f-8841-3f22646e5074" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8914 :tutorial/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7cd53e1d-4c0c-570b-bef4-68f8081c2fef" :step/math-academy-id 191938 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134612}} {:db/id 17592186135192 :tutorial/content "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the arithmetic sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}1},\\, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}6},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1?\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}6},$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =6+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =7-6 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $a_1 = {\\color{blue}6}$ and $d={\\color{red}1}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n&= {\\color{blue}6} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}1}) \\[3pt]\n& =6 + n-1\\[3pt]\n&=n+5\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=n+5, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c5b9f8-4c71-5c1f-8841-3f22646e5074" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8914 :tutorial/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"} {:db/id 17592186134612 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133533 :question/id #uuid "d732cfb5-9db5-535d-adb1-5d3292f1c497" :question/math-academy-id "e-14389"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e1e439-ec4a-5577-80d5-3b113bf36e74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134056} {:db/id 17592186134164} {:db/id 17592186233564} {:db/id 17592186233571} {:db/id 17592186233578} {:db/id 17592186560995} {:db/id 17592186561033}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "2a238f59-a780-5248-bfa5-a217bec91d6c" :step/math-academy-id 191939 :step/next {:db/id 17592186134613}} {:db/id 17592186134613 :step/content {:db/id 17592186133749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186133534 :question/id #uuid "26b3b89d-d485-51ac-b964-8377ce3aff28" :question/math-academy-id "e-14393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baac268a-629e-5887-885a-49393c72776f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186134178} {:db/id 17592186134479} {:db/id 17592186560974} {:db/id 17592186561053}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "c81f0b63-4b36-56b0-bc0f-1535d35453ef" :step/math-academy-id 191943} {:activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186079457} :activity/id #uuid "45c505a4-630e-59ff-9596-9a36627fb33e" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186050396 :topic/id #uuid "457136ef-67f9-50bb-b078-34de9c6b59a9" :topic/math-academy-id 655} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186079457 :step/content {:db/id 17592186079621 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial term** is a term where the variable contains a non-negative integer power. (The non-negative integers consist of the numbers $0$, $1$, $2$, $3$, and so on.)\n\nSome examples of polynomial terms include\n\n$$\nx^2, \\qquad \\dfrac 1 3 x^6,\\qquad x, \\qquad 1\n$$\n\nLet's go through each one:\n\n- $x^{\\color{blue}2}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}2}$, a non-negative integer.\n- $\\dfrac{1}{3} x^{\\color{blue}6}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}6}$, which is also a non-negative integer. The coefficient of $\\dfrac 1 3$ isn't important.\n- $x$ is a polynomial term because it can be thought of as $x^{\\color{blue}1}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}1}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n- Finally, $1$ is a polynomial term because it can be expressed as $1\\cdot x^{\\color{blue}0}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}0}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n\nOn the other hand, the following expressions are *not* polynomial terms because their powers are not non-negative integers:\n\n$$\n4x^{-2}, \\qquad x^{1/3}, \\qquad {\\sqrt{\\color{black}x}}, \\qquad {\\dfrac{\\color{black}1}{x^3}}\n$$\n\nTo see why, let's go through each one:\n\n- $4x^{\\color{red}-2}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-2}$, which is negative.\n- $x^{\\color{red}1/3}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/3}$, which is not an integer.\n- $\\sqrt x$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{\\color{red}1/2}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/2}$, which is not an integer.\n- Finally, $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{-\\color{red}3}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-3}$, which is negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "a11e9fb3-6fe1-547c-996f-fb83d15a47d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1578 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0500aa37-469a-53bd-a5da-8fbb0fe569cc" :step/math-academy-id 4106 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079458}} {:db/id 17592186079458 :step/content {:db/id 17592186078529 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186078153 :question/id #uuid "0690af9e-ae28-50a0-b2fd-0a9c3baaec3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1280"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c12f648d-f276-5123-b6aa-b222ad108825" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049089} {:db/id 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186079068} {:db/id 17592186079149}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polynomial Terms"} :step/id #uuid "ad32752d-72d3-5211-adbf-f50f0a51075b" :step/math-academy-id 4107 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079459}} {:db/id 17592186079459 :step/content {:db/id 17592186079622 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial** is a sum of polynomial terms, such as\n$x^2 -5x +3, \\qquad x^3 + x^2 - 9x, \\qquad 4x^4 -2x^2, \\qquad 3x^6 - 2x^3+x^2-1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b356365-33dd-5603-8027-95f09e3184a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1579 :tutorial/title "Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "a43ff34d-c027-5df2-820c-70b022c83dc9" :step/math-academy-id 4109 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079460}} {:db/id 17592186079460 :step/content {:db/id 17592186078530 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186078155 :question/id #uuid "f6c0be28-9441-513e-83c5-866292fd307a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df8ab577-24c5-5ac4-a9de-9ecc7dfb6653" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186078922} {:db/id 17592186079069}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "2556eb4e-afab-5ae3-8996-7aa3b8f721fc" :step/math-academy-id 4112 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079461}} {:db/id 17592186079461 :step/content {:db/id 17592186079623 :tutorial/content "To **evaluate** a polynomial at a specific value of $x$, we substitute the value of $x$ into the polynomial and simplify.\n\nFor example, to evaluate the polynomial $2x^2+3$ at $x=5$, we substitute $x=5$ into the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(5)^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(25) + 3 &=  \\\\\n50 + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 53\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8de8488-40d3-593a-a8a1-9193eb94f8e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1580 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "64a56fd4-dcbc-5271-8a10-da5817638249" :step/math-academy-id 4114 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079462}} {:db/id 17592186079462 :step/content {:db/id 17592186078531 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186078162 :question/id #uuid "5242547e-f74d-541d-8c3b-def606b70cb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1283"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f7a2ec-3d90-5930-bcc0-d51b8111dbe7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186078853} {:db/id 17592186079150} {:db/id 17592186459300} {:db/id 17592186480647} {:db/id 17592186487324} {:db/id 17592186491165} {:db/id 17592186498565} {:db/id 17592186550022}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "f4dd171c-5f47-5387-8772-afefbec23a35" :step/math-academy-id 4115 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079463}} {:db/id 17592186079463 :step/content {:db/id 17592186078532 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186078163 :question/id #uuid "7dc04a57-3d13-5482-93c2-db2cee4ca41a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1284"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2229bd-c425-5e53-b8cf-56f3f9f3f539" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186078927} {:db/id 17592186079151} {:db/id 17592186233558} {:db/id 17592186233565} {:db/id 17592186233572} {:db/id 17592186485499} {:db/id 17592186485579}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Polynomial Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "666b2e7a-bdbf-55bd-a1ea-25b21b4d56f8" :step/math-academy-id 4117}] :activity/title "Introduction to Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186078677} {:db/id 17592186079457 :step/content {:db/id 17592186079621 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial term** is a term where the variable contains a non-negative integer power. (The non-negative integers consist of the numbers $0$, $1$, $2$, $3$, and so on.)\n\nSome examples of polynomial terms include\n\n$$\nx^2, \\qquad \\dfrac 1 3 x^6,\\qquad x, \\qquad 1\n$$\n\nLet's go through each one:\n\n- $x^{\\color{blue}2}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}2}$, a non-negative integer.\n- $\\dfrac{1}{3} x^{\\color{blue}6}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}6}$, which is also a non-negative integer. The coefficient of $\\dfrac 1 3$ isn't important.\n- $x$ is a polynomial term because it can be thought of as $x^{\\color{blue}1}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}1}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n- Finally, $1$ is a polynomial term because it can be expressed as $1\\cdot x^{\\color{blue}0}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}0}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n\nOn the other hand, the following expressions are *not* polynomial terms because their powers are not non-negative integers:\n\n$$\n4x^{-2}, \\qquad x^{1/3}, \\qquad {\\sqrt{\\color{black}x}}, \\qquad {\\dfrac{\\color{black}1}{x^3}}\n$$\n\nTo see why, let's go through each one:\n\n- $4x^{\\color{red}-2}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-2}$, which is negative.\n- $x^{\\color{red}1/3}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/3}$, which is not an integer.\n- $\\sqrt x$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{\\color{red}1/2}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/2}$, which is not an integer.\n- Finally, $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{-\\color{red}3}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-3}$, which is negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "a11e9fb3-6fe1-547c-996f-fb83d15a47d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1578 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0500aa37-469a-53bd-a5da-8fbb0fe569cc" :step/math-academy-id 4106 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079458}} {:db/id 17592186079621 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial term** is a term where the variable contains a non-negative integer power. (The non-negative integers consist of the numbers $0$, $1$, $2$, $3$, and so on.)\n\nSome examples of polynomial terms include\n\n$$\nx^2, \\qquad \\dfrac 1 3 x^6,\\qquad x, \\qquad 1\n$$\n\nLet's go through each one:\n\n- $x^{\\color{blue}2}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}2}$, a non-negative integer.\n- $\\dfrac{1}{3} x^{\\color{blue}6}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}6}$, which is also a non-negative integer. The coefficient of $\\dfrac 1 3$ isn't important.\n- $x$ is a polynomial term because it can be thought of as $x^{\\color{blue}1}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}1}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n- Finally, $1$ is a polynomial term because it can be expressed as $1\\cdot x^{\\color{blue}0}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}0}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n\nOn the other hand, the following expressions are *not* polynomial terms because their powers are not non-negative integers:\n\n$$\n4x^{-2}, \\qquad x^{1/3}, \\qquad {\\sqrt{\\color{black}x}}, \\qquad {\\dfrac{\\color{black}1}{x^3}}\n$$\n\nTo see why, let's go through each one:\n\n- $4x^{\\color{red}-2}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-2}$, which is negative.\n- $x^{\\color{red}1/3}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/3}$, which is not an integer.\n- $\\sqrt x$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{\\color{red}1/2}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/2}$, which is not an integer.\n- Finally, $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{-\\color{red}3}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-3}$, which is negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "a11e9fb3-6fe1-547c-996f-fb83d15a47d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1578 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186079458 :step/content {:db/id 17592186078529 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186078153 :question/id #uuid "0690af9e-ae28-50a0-b2fd-0a9c3baaec3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1280"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c12f648d-f276-5123-b6aa-b222ad108825" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049089} {:db/id 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186079068} {:db/id 17592186079149}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polynomial Terms"} :step/id #uuid "ad32752d-72d3-5211-adbf-f50f0a51075b" :step/math-academy-id 4107 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079459}} {:db/id 17592186079459 :step/content {:db/id 17592186079622 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial** is a sum of polynomial terms, such as\n$x^2 -5x +3, \\qquad x^3 + x^2 - 9x, \\qquad 4x^4 -2x^2, \\qquad 3x^6 - 2x^3+x^2-1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b356365-33dd-5603-8027-95f09e3184a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1579 :tutorial/title "Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "a43ff34d-c027-5df2-820c-70b022c83dc9" :step/math-academy-id 4109 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079460}} {:db/id 17592186079622 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial** is a sum of polynomial terms, such as\n$x^2 -5x +3, \\qquad x^3 + x^2 - 9x, \\qquad 4x^4 -2x^2, \\qquad 3x^6 - 2x^3+x^2-1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b356365-33dd-5603-8027-95f09e3184a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1579 :tutorial/title "Polynomials"} {:db/id 17592186079460 :step/content {:db/id 17592186078530 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186078155 :question/id #uuid "f6c0be28-9441-513e-83c5-866292fd307a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df8ab577-24c5-5ac4-a9de-9ecc7dfb6653" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186078922} {:db/id 17592186079069}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "2556eb4e-afab-5ae3-8996-7aa3b8f721fc" :step/math-academy-id 4112 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079461}} {:db/id 17592186079461 :step/content {:db/id 17592186079623 :tutorial/content "To **evaluate** a polynomial at a specific value of $x$, we substitute the value of $x$ into the polynomial and simplify.\n\nFor example, to evaluate the polynomial $2x^2+3$ at $x=5$, we substitute $x=5$ into the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(5)^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(25) + 3 &=  \\\\\n50 + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 53\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8de8488-40d3-593a-a8a1-9193eb94f8e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1580 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "64a56fd4-dcbc-5271-8a10-da5817638249" :step/math-academy-id 4114 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079462}} {:db/id 17592186079623 :tutorial/content "To **evaluate** a polynomial at a specific value of $x$, we substitute the value of $x$ into the polynomial and simplify.\n\nFor example, to evaluate the polynomial $2x^2+3$ at $x=5$, we substitute $x=5$ into the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(5)^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(25) + 3 &=  \\\\\n50 + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 53\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8de8488-40d3-593a-a8a1-9193eb94f8e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1580 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Polynomials"} {:db/id 17592186079462 :step/content {:db/id 17592186078531 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186078162 :question/id #uuid "5242547e-f74d-541d-8c3b-def606b70cb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1283"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f7a2ec-3d90-5930-bcc0-d51b8111dbe7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186078853} {:db/id 17592186079150} {:db/id 17592186459300} {:db/id 17592186480647} {:db/id 17592186487324} {:db/id 17592186491165} {:db/id 17592186498565} {:db/id 17592186550022}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "f4dd171c-5f47-5387-8772-afefbec23a35" :step/math-academy-id 4115 :step/next {:db/id 17592186079463}} {:db/id 17592186079463 :step/content {:db/id 17592186078532 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186078163 :question/id #uuid "7dc04a57-3d13-5482-93c2-db2cee4ca41a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1284"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2229bd-c425-5e53-b8cf-56f3f9f3f539" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186078927} {:db/id 17592186079151} {:db/id 17592186233558} {:db/id 17592186233565} {:db/id 17592186233572} {:db/id 17592186485499} {:db/id 17592186485579}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Polynomial Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "666b2e7a-bdbf-55bd-a1ea-25b21b4d56f8" :step/math-academy-id 4117} {:activity/course {:db/id 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186485641} :activity/id #uuid "c520d89b-b214-53fa-952a-5cdaa5d839b2" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186050396 :topic/id #uuid "457136ef-67f9-50bb-b078-34de9c6b59a9" :topic/math-academy-id 655} :acti