lue}x$ on one side of the equation:  \n\nIn this case, we apply the addition principle and add $7{\\color{red}y}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-7y & =1 \\\\ x-7y+7y & =1+7y \\\\ x+0 & =1+7y \\\\ x & =1+7y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully isolated the variable ${\\color{blue}{x}}.$\n\nLet's now look at an example where we must apply the multiplication principle." :tutorial/id #uuid "c431a781-752f-59ae-bf37-616b43771b6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8072 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c59fe348-2f63-5607-9155-2839edb15553" :step/math-academy-id 72728 :step/next {:db/id 17592186065267}} {:db/id 17592186065414 :tutorial/content "Equations can contain more than one variable. For example, the following equation has two variables, $\\color{blue}x$ and ${\\color{red}y}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x} - 7{\\color{red}y} = 1\n$$\n\nTo **solve the equation for the variable ** ${\\color{blue}{x}},$ we need to isolate $\\color{blue}x$ on one side of the equation:  \n\nIn this case, we apply the addition principle and add $7{\\color{red}y}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-7y & =1 \\\\ x-7y+7y & =1+7y \\\\ x+0 & =1+7y \\\\ x & =1+7y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully isolated the variable ${\\color{blue}{x}}.$\n\nLet's now look at an example where we must apply the multiplication principle." :tutorial/id #uuid "c431a781-752f-59ae-bf37-616b43771b6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8072 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186065267 :step/content {:db/id 17592186064347 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186063937 :question/id #uuid "46597138-36cc-584b-ade8-70cbfafcad2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97dc4044-9984-58a3-bf02-61f31fc31792" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048725} {:db/id 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186064498} {:db/id 17592186064787} {:db/id 17592186064788} {:db/id 17592186488192} {:db/id 1759218648825