 can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + 3x + 4x_(⏟)_(7x) + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$" 17592186045417] [17592186056069 1065 8891 17592186045417] [17592186056069 1066 #uuid "90a8aa34-4549-57c2-bdc2-24cc962cd306" 17592186045417] [17592186056069 1067 "Introduction" 17592186045417] [17592186056070 1064 "Let's now consider the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 8\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term $(-8)$ is *negative*. So, when writing down our factor pairs, one number will always be positive, and the other will always be negative.\n\nSo, first, we write down all factor pairs of $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-8), (-1, 8), (2,-4), (-2, 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-8) &= -7 & \\cdot \\\\ (-1) + 8|= 7 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + (-4)|=-2 & ✓ \\\\ (-2) + 4|= 2 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-2x = 2x-4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (-4x - 8) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 2) - 4(x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 17592186045417] [17592186056070 1065 8894 17592186045417] [17592186056070 1066 #uuid "3b5a5752-232b-5714-b74a-1e50c4210b83" 17592186045417] [17592186056070 1067 "Trinomials With Negative Constants" 17592186045417] [17592186056076 1064 "Remember that to factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the constant term that adds to the coefficient of the linear term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 7x+12\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the constant term $(12)$ is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the constant is *positive* and the coefficient of the linear term is *negative*, we must first consider the *negative* factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$. These are as follows:\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\qquad (-2,-6),\\qquad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their *sum* is negative, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) + (-12) &= -13 & \\cdot \\\\ (-2) + (-6)|=-8 & \\cdot \\\\ (-3) + (-4)|=-7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-7x = -3x - 4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 7x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 3x) + (-4x + 12) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3) + 4(-x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to get a *common* factor in both terms, we take an additional factor of $-1$ from the second term:\n\n$$\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3)\n$$\n\nNow that we have a common factor of $(x-3)$, we factor the expression fully in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 3)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 17592186045417] [17592186056076 1065 9504 17592186045417] [17592186056076 1066 #uuid "25f481d5-38d8-5f35-95fc-dd74f97d0394" 17592186045417] [17592186056076 1067 "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants" 17592186045417] [17592186064322 1038 17592186064684 17592186045417] [17592186064322 1038 17592186064720 17592186045417] [17592186064322 1039 "Factoring a Monomial out of a Binomial Containing Two Variables" 17592186045417] [17592186064322 1041 17592186063953 17592186045417] [17592186064322 1042 #uuid "12fed420-dbc6-57b4-8b3b-a13d441a51df" 17592186045417] [17592186064323 1038 17592186064675 17592186045417] [17592186064323 1038 17592186064719 17592186045417] [17592186064323 1038 17592186233562 17592186045417] [17592186064323 1038 17592186233569 17592186045417] [17592186064323 1038 17592186233576 17592186045417] [17592186064323 1039 "Factoring a Monomial From a Trinomial" 17592186045417] [17592186064323 1041 17592186063954 17592186045417] [17592186064323 1042 #uuid "6d1134c0-02ce-59c1-8238-4820da388f90" 17592186045417] [17592186064324 1038 17592186064901 17592186045417] [17592186064324 1038 17592186064902 17592186045417] [17592186064324 1039 "Factoring a Binomial" 17592186045417] [17592186064324 1041 17592186063955 17592186045417] [17592186064324 1042 #uuid "201a17a5-fbf3-586d-83b1-73c3f8b643fb" 17592186045417] [17592186064341 1037 17592186050526 17592186045417] [17592186064341 1037 17592186051079 17592186045417] [17592186064341 1038 17592186064674 17592186045417] [17592186064341 1038 17592186064721 17592186045417] [17592186064341 1038 17592186459231 17592186